{
  "dom_id": "step-q237311",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The population of ducks living in a nature reserve triples every year. If there were $8$ ducks initially, how many years will it take for the population to increase to $5\\,832?$\n\nThe population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after{{field-1}}years.",
  "worked_solution": "We know that the duck population triples every year. Thus, the duck population, which we will call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $3.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=8$ is the initial population of ducks.\n\n- $b=3$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=8\\cdot {3}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the population of ducks to increase to $5\\,832,$ we need to solve the equation $y(t)=5\\,832$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\,832 \\\\ 8\\cdot {3}^{t} & =5\\,832 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =\\frac{5\\,832}{8} \\\\ {3}^{t} & =729 \\\\ {3}^{t} & ={3}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after $6$ years.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
